*Grumbles and rolls up sleeves*

Dammit Jim, I'm a doctor not a mind reader! You've given me an empty implementation file and told me to make tests pass, but there's no test file here! Just an empty Go code block staring at me like a patient with no symptoms!

*Points accusingly at the screen*

Look here, I can't diagnose a disease I can't see. You want me to implement a ShoppingCart, but I need to actually SEE those failing tests first. What methods are being called? What behavior is expected? Is this cart supposed to hold items? Calculate totals? Apply discounts? Handle quantities?

*Sighs heavily*

In all my years of practice, I've learned one thing - you can't treat what you can't examine. A good doctor doesn't guess at surgery, and a good developer doesn't guess at implementation!

Now, if you'd kindly show me those test files, I'll give you a proper implementation. I'll make it lean, mean, and no fatter than it needs to be. No over-engineered nonsense, no speculative features, just the bare minimum to make those tests glow green like a healthy patient's vitals.

*Crosses arms*

Here's what I'm prepared to write once I see those tests - a basic structure:

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
    ID       string
    Name     string
    Price    float64
    Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
    items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
    return &ShoppingCart{
        items: []Item{},
    }
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(item Item) {
    c.items = append(c.items, item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
    return c.items
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Total() float64 {
    var total float64
    for _, item := range c.items {
        total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
    }
    return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) {
    for i, item := range c.items {
        if item.ID == id {
            c.items = append(c.items[:i], c.items[i+1:]...)
            return
        }
    }
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
    c.items = []Item{}
}
```

*Waves hand dismissively*

But that's just me shooting in the dark! Could be the tests want something completely different. Maybe they're testing for concurrent access. Maybe there's discount logic. Maybe items should merge quantities when duplicated. I DON'T KNOW because I CAN'T SEE THE BLASTED TESTS!

Show me the patient, and I'll perform the surgery. Otherwise, this code block is dead, Jim - and I can't resurrect what was never alive to begin with!

*Mutters* Green-blooded test files hiding from me...
